    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a serious plateau on their back squat. they haven't added any weight in 3 weeks and their RPE is hitting 10 way too early. can you explain how deload weeks and changing up the volume might help them break through this? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A deload week, by significantly reducing the load, is often the perfect antidote to a plateau, as it allows the nervous system to recover from the constant strain of heavy lifting. Think of it like a planned \"rest week\" for your muscles, which can sometimes be more beneficial than just waiting for the fatigue to dissipate naturally. For your client, after the deload, you can then strategically change up the volume, perhaps by shifting from high-rep sets to lower-rep, heavier singles or doubles to shock the system. This variation prevents the body from adapting to the same stimulus repeatedly, which is what causes plateaus.",
      "source": "target",
