    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, so it's more like a protective mechanism. but it still feels so random sometimes, like they do everything right and then it's just... stuck. is there a way to predict when this stalling is going to happen before it actually happens, or is it always just a surprise once they hit the wall?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Predicting the exact moment a plateau hits is nearly impossible, as individual recovery rates are highly variable, but you can certainly look for warning signs. These signs are often subtle, like a persistent, low-grade feeling of sluggishness, or perhaps their sleep quality starts declining even when they aren't overly fatigued.\n\nYou can also monitor performance metrics beyond just the weight lifted; look at their RPE consistency—if their RPE is consistently 8 when they used to be hitting 6 for the same weight, that's a red flag. These subtle shifts are your early indicators that the system is starting to become overloaded, much like a small, persistent drip of water eroding a dam before a major breach.",
      "source": "target",
